re.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s208258/appendix-1-annual-governance-statement-2025-26.pdf","committee":"Audit and Standards Committee","date":"07/07/2026","key":true},{"title":"7. Agenda Item 7 (26 June 2026) Business Plan Outturn Report 2025-2026","url":"https://Staffordshire.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s208115/7-agenda-item-7-26-june-2026-business-plan-outturn-report-20.pdf","committee":"Pensions Committee","date":"26/06/2026","key":false}],"read_documents":[],"calendar":{"upcoming":[{"date":"2026-09-25","committee":"Pensions Committee","time":"10:00am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://staffordshire.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-25","committee":"Pensions Board","time":"1:30pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://staffordshire.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-28","committee":"County Council","time":"10:00am","status":"Confirmed; Extraordinary Meeting","webcast":true,"url":"https://staffordshire.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-29","committee":"Audit and Standards Committee","time":"10:00am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://staffordshire.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-05","committee":"Corporate Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"10:00am","status":"Provisional; Reserved for call-in","webcast":true,"url":"https://staffordshire.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-08","committee":"County Council","time":"10:00am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://staffordshire.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-15","committee":"Safeguarding and Education Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"10:00am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://staffordshire.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-19","committee":"Health and Care Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"10:00am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://staffordshire.moderngov.co.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-09-08","committee":"Corporate Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"10:00am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://staffordshire.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-10","committee":"Safeguarding and Education Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"10:00am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://staffordshire.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-14","committee":"Health and Care Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"10:00am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://staffordshire.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-16","committee":"Cabinet","time":"10:00am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://staffordshire.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-24","committee":"Economy, Infrastructure and Communities Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"10:00am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://staffordshire.moderngov.co.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Staffordshire's street lighting contract, due to expire in May 2028, faces critical planning gaps despite sound current operation","lead":"Staffordshire County Council's 25-year street lighting PFI contract with EON has been well-run, with lights availability consistently exceeding 99 per cent against a 98 per cent target. However, the council faces significant unpreparedness as the contract approaches expiry in May 2028. An Infrastructure and Projects Authority assessment rates the council's readiness as Amber, requiring moderate additional work to address significant issues. The council has no transition plan in place, has not decided on a future delivery model, and faces unknown costs including purchasing EON's depots and equipment, remediation of approximately 3,000 to 4,000 ageing assets, and potential employment liabilities for around 25 staff. The council must also refinance or repay a \u00a31 million project debt by September 2025. Key staff, including the contract manager, are retiring as the handover period approaches.","iso":"2026-09-24","paper_title":"Appendix 1 \u2013 Proving Services, Staffordshire Street Lighting PFI Service: Value for Money Assessment v2 (May 2025).","paper_url":"https://staffordshire.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s209503/209503.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-29","committee":"Audit and Standards Committee","paper_title":"Appendix 1 - 2026-27 Internal Audit Plan - Progress Update","paper_url":"https://staffordshire.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s209831/209831.pdf","headline":"Chief Audit Officer position vacant since February 2026; responsibilities shared between two managers and Assistant Director","detail":"The Chief Internal Auditor departed in February 2026. From April 2026, responsibilities have been shared between two Audit Managers (acting as Senior Audit Managers) and the Assistant Director for Audit, Risk and Insurance & Financial Services. An honorarium is being paid for the additional responsibilities, indicating this is a temporary arrangement. This represents a 7-month period without permanent leadership of the audit function.","category":"governance","matters":"The audit function is critical to governance and internal control. Prolonged vacancy in this leadership role could affect audit quality and the council's ability to identify control weaknesses. A lender would consider audit leadership stability as part of assessing governance quality.","figures":"Vacancy since February 2026; shared arrangement from April 2026 onwards (5+ months as of 16 September 2026)","quote":"Following the departure of the previous Chief Internal Auditor in February 2026, Chief Internal Auditor responsibilities have been shared between two Audit Managers, who have been acting as Senior Audit Managers since April 2026, and the Assistant Director for Audit, Risk and Insurance & Financial Services.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-29","committee":"Audit and Standards Committee","paper_title":"Appendix 1 - 2026-27 Internal Audit Plan - Progress Update","paper_url":"https://staffordshire.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s209831/209831.pdf","headline":"One high-priority audit recommendation on Dedicated Schools Grant High Needs Block debt management remains overdue since July 2026 with no progress updates","detail":"A high-priority recommendation due 31 July 2026 required the council to review and update the DSG High Needs Block debt position analysis and establish clear arrangements for reporting and managing debt risks. The original weakness was that 'The High Need Block debt position had not been recently reviewed, and the methods for reporting debts and managing associated risks was not clear.' As of 16 September 2026, this remains unimplemented with no progress updates provided by management.","category":"audit","matters":"A lender requires confidence the council understands and manages its material financial liabilities. The DSG High Needs Block is a significant budget area typically under financial pressure; an unimplemented high-priority recommendation on debt management suggests the council may lack adequate visibility over a material liability.","figures":"1 high-priority recommendation overdue; due 31/07/2026 (6+ weeks overdue); only 1 of 36 corporate high-priority recommendations overdue","quote":"For corporate areas, progress in implementing those outstanding agreed recommendations has been monitored and only one high-priority level recommendation remains overdue at the time of reporting (16th September 2026).","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-29","committee":"Audit and Standards Committee","paper_title":"Draft Value for Money Opinion (2025/26) - Staffordshire County Council","paper_url":"https://staffordshire.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s209809/209809.pdf","headline":"General fund balances improved and the \u00a315m contingency budget remained unspent, strengthening short-term resilience.","detail":"General fund balances increased compared to 31 March 2025, continuing a positive trend. The \u00a315m contingency budget was not drawn and will transfer to the capital reserve. Reserve balances were actively strengthened in Highways, Waste, Public Health and a new Local Government Reorganisation reserve, providing additional resilience for future pressures.","category":"reserves","matters":"Despite emerging pressures, the Council's general reserve position is improving and contingency budgets remain unspent, providing short-to-medium-term flexibility to absorb the Children's Services overspend.","figures":"\u00a315m contingency unspent; overall outturn underspend \u00a36.696m on revised budget \u00a3753.1m","quote":"The Council increased its general fund balances compared to 31 March 2025, continuing the trend from the previous year. The \u00a315m contingency budget was not used and will be transferred to the capital reserve for future capital investments in line with the MTFS.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-29","committee":"Audit and Standards Committee","paper_title":"Draft Value for Money Opinion (2025/26) - Staffordshire County Council","paper_url":"https://staffordshire.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s209809/209809.pdf","headline":"Members' conduct complaints increased 14-fold year-on-year, from 12 to 168 complaints.","detail":"The Council received 168 complaints against Elected Members between May 2025 and April 2026, compared to 12 in the prior year. The Council has introduced operational changes to strengthen consistency and oversight, with officers providing guidance and training to support high standards of conduct.","category":"governance","matters":"A dramatic increase in conduct complaints suggests potential changes in governance standards or culture requiring management attention.","figures":"168 complaints (May 2025\u2013April 2026); 12 complaints (prior year); 14x increase","quote":"Between the 2nd May 2025 and 30th April 2026, 168 complaints were received against Elected Members of Staffordshire County Council. This represents a significant increase compared with the same period in 2024/25, when 12 complaints were received.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-29","committee":"Audit and Standards Committee","paper_title":"Draft Value for Money Opinion (2025/26) - Staffordshire County Council","paper_url":"https://staffordshire.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s209809/209809.pdf","headline":"The Council is failing to meet the statutory 20-week timeframe for Education, Health and Care Plans, achieving only 23.2% compliance at Q3 2025/26.","detail":"The Council is not meeting statutory requirements to complete EHCPs within 20 weeks under the Children and Families Act 2024. Q3 2025/26 performance was 23.2%, compared to a Council target of 49% and national average of 46.4%. The statutory requirement is 100%. This is flagged as a Priority One performance improvement observation.","category":"governance","matters":"Failure to meet statutory requirements is a regulatory and reputational risk, signalling service delivery challenges in SEND, the same area driving major budget pressures through DSG deficits.","figures":"Q3 2025/26: 23.2% compliance; Council target: 49%; national average: 46.4%; statutory requirement: 100%","quote":"The Council is not meeting the statutory 20-week timeframe for completing Education, Health and Care Plans (EHCPs), as required by the Children and Families Act 2024.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-24"},"name":"Staffordshire CC","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-25","since_iso":"2026-08-26","distress":false,"headline":"Contract has been well-managed with excellent operational performance; asset availability at 99%+ versus 98% target","lead":"Strong operational performance and contractor capability reduces execution risk during transition despite the governance, planning and resource challenges identified elsewhere in the assessment.","also":["11 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Audit and Standards Committee on 2026-09-29","n_findings":12,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Appendix 1 \u2013 Proving Services, Staffordshire Street Lighting PFI Service: Value for Money Assessment v2 (May 2025).","top_paper_url":"https://staffordshire.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s209503/209503.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":121,"news_drafts_total":1}}